            <ArticleTitle>Anaphylactic reaction to tranexamic acid in an adolescent undergoing posterior spinal fusion.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>774-775</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/pan.13141</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Tranexamic acid is an anti-fibrinolytic agent frequently used in pediatric surgery. Common side effects include nausea, flushing, and headache, but in rare instances, it may produce anaphylaxis; with only one previously reported case in a 72-year-old man. We report a case of a delayed anaphylactic reaction in a pediatric patient undergoing posterior spine fusion; and discuss the intraoperative management of the acute event, immunologic confirmation, and subsequent anesthetic approach.</AbstractText>
